<description>
The `JS_ParseJSON` function parses a JSON string and returns a `JSValue`. It is a wrapper around `JS_ParseJSON2`, which performs the actual parsing. The function first initializes a `JSParseState` with the input buffer and then iteratively parses the JSON tokens. It handles various JSON data types, including objects, arrays, strings, numbers, booleans, and null.
</description>

<requirements>
<requirement>
The `ctx` argument must be a valid pointer to a `JSContext` object. This is because the function internally uses the context to allocate memory and handle exceptions.
</requirement>
<requirement>
The `buf` argument must be a valid pointer to a character buffer containing the JSON string. This is because the function reads from this buffer to parse the JSON data.
</requirement>
<requirement>
The `buf_len` argument must be the correct length of the `buf` buffer. An incorrect length can lead to out-of-bounds memory access during parsing.
</requirement>
<requirement>
The `filename` argument can be a string literal. It is used for error reporting and does not affect the parsing logic.
</requirement>
</requirements>

</function-requirements>

